[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The outage is caused by router1’s peering interface (eth0) toward router2 being down. On router1, eth0 is administratively/operationally down: “ip link show dev eth0” reports state DOWN (no link ups), and ethtool shows “Link detected: no.” With eth0 down, router1 loses the connected route to 193.10.11.0/24, cannot ARP for 193.10.11.2, and cannot form eBGP. Consequently, “show ip bgp summary” on router1 shows the neighbor 193.10.11.2 stuck in Active with 0 prefixes. Router2 corroborates this: its neighbor 193.10.11.1 is Active with a recent “Hold Timer Expired” reset, and it cannot reach 193.10.11.1 (ICMP Destination Host Unreachable). End-to-end, pc1->pc2 fails with “From 195.11.14.1 Destination Net Unreachable,” which is router1 signaling it lacks a route to 200.1.1.0/24 (because eBGP is down).

Localization: the physical/link layer on router1 eth0 (193.10.11.1/24) toward router2 eth0 (193.10.11.2/24). Router2’s eth0 is up (“Link detected: yes”), and its LAN to pc2 works, so router2 is not the culprit.

Root cause: router1’s eth0 interface toward the peer was taken down or lost carrier, breaking the eBGP session and transit.

Corrective fix: Bring router1’s eth0 back up (e.g., ip link set eth0 up or restart router1’s networking). Once eth0 is up, the eBGP session will reestablish and routes to 200.1.1.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24 will be exchanged. If needed, restart FRR on router1 after the link is restored. Do not restart router2.
=== END ===